                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+conjecture"} Poincaré conjecture
                        %li
                          %p
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptization+conjecture"} elliptization conjecture
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"}> cobordism hypothesis
                        \-theorem
                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/manifolds+and+cobordisms+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#stein_manifold"} Stein manifold
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#stein_spaces"} Stein spaces
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#basic_properties"} Basic properties
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#GoodCoversBySteinManifolds"} Good covers by Stein manifolds
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#Cohomology"} Cohomology
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#RelationToAffineVarieties"} Relation to affine varieties
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#okagrauert_principle"} Oka-Grauert principle
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em Stein manifold
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+manifold"} complex manifold
            satisfying some niceness conditions generalizing the concept of a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain+of+holomorphy"} domain of holomorphy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+functions"} holomorphic functions
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+axiom"} separate points
          %p
            From the point of view of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+manifolds"} complex manifolds
            much as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
            are to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+manifold"} complex manifold
            has a “good
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} cover
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
            with values in any analytic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+sheaf"} coherent sheaf
            on any Stein manifold vanishes (this is
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan%27s+theorem+B"} Cartan's theorem B
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#CartanTheoremB"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covers"} covers
            of complex manifolds by Stein manifolds usual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cech+cohomology"} Cech cohomology
            techniques work for analytic coherent sheaves (such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+sheaf"} structure sheaf
            of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+functions"} holomorphic functions
          %p
            Accordingly, Stein spaces are close to being the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+varieties"} affine varieties
            over the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#RelationToAffineVarieties"} below
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#stein_manifold Stein manifold
          %p
            The original definition (
            %a{:href => "#Stein51"}> Stein 51
            ) says that a
            %em Stein manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+manifold"} complex manifold
            which is a “holomorphically convex” and “holomorphically separable” subset of a
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n
          %p
            This is equivalent to (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reinhold+Remmert"}> Reinhold Remmert
            , Narasimhan, Bishop) saying that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            admits a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+morphism"} proper
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+function"} holomorphic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/immersion"} immersion
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ↪
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \hookrightarrow\mathbb{C}^n
          %h3#stein_spaces Stein spaces
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+analytic+space"} complex analytic space
            is a
            %em Stein space
            precisely if its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduction+modality"} reduction
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stein+manifold"} Stein manifold
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain+of+holomorphy"} domain of holomorphy
              in some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n
              is a Stein manifold.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              Every closed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submanifold"} sub-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+manifold"} complex manifold
              of a Stein manifold is itself Stein.
          %p
          #SteinSurfacesAreOpenRiemannSurfaces.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong (Stein surfaces are open Riemann surfaces)
              %br/
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+topological+space"} connected
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stein+manifold"} Stein manifold
              if and only if it is open (i.e. not
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
          (
          %a{:href => "#BehnkeStein47"}> Behnke &amp; Stein 1947
          , review in
          = succeed ")" do
            %a{:href => "#Wehler20"} Wehler 2020, 15.3
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#basic_properties Basic properties
          %p
            A Stein manifold is necessarily a non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"}> compact topological space
            \.
          %h3#GoodCoversBySteinManifolds Good covers by Stein manifolds
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+manifold"} complex manifold
            admits a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/good+cover"} good cover
            by Stein manifolds, in the sense that all finite non-empty intersections of the cover are Stein manifolds (e.g.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Maddock09"} Maddock 09, lemma 3.2.8
            %em not
            in the sense that these intersections are contractible! Rather, all Dolbeault cohomology in positive degree vanishes.
          %h3#Cohomology Cohomology
          %p
            The following central property of Stein manifolds is due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henri+Cartan"} Henri Cartan
          #CartanTheoremB.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan%27s+theorem+B"} Cartan's theorem B
            %p
              On a Stein manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              and for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              an analytic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+sheaf"} coherent sheaf
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              then all the
              = succeed "-degree" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
              groups of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              vanish:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo •
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    H^{\bullet \geq 1}(\Sigma, A) = 0
                    \\,.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Serre54"}> Serre 1954
            , Thm. B on
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/SB_1951-1954__2__213_0.pdf#page=3"} p. 214
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Forstneric11"} Forstnerič 2011, theorem 2.4.1
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              Also all
              = succeed "-degree" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dolbeault+cohomology"} Dolbeault cohomology
              groups vanish:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi H
                      %mover
                        %mo ∂
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                      %mrow
                        %mo •
                        %mo ,
                        %mo •
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    H^{\bullet, \bullet \geq 1}_{\bar \partial}(\Sigma) = 0
                    \\,.
          %p
            (recalled in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Forstneric11"} Forstnerič 2011, theorem 2.4.6
          %p As an important special case, it follows that:
          %p
          #HolomorphicDeRhamCoincidesWithDeRhamOnSteinMfds.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+de+Rham+cohomology"} holomorphic de Rham cohomology
              of Stein manifolds coincides with ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
              (with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
          (
          %a{:href => "#Serre54"}> Serre 1954 §2.1
          , cf. also
          = succeed ")" do
            %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/q/273178/381"} MO:q/273178
          %h3#RelationToAffineVarieties Relation to affine varieties
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytification"} analytification
            of any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+variety"} affine variety
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            is a Stein space, but the converse is not quite true. (
            %a{:href => "#Zhang06"}> Zhang 06
            ). There is also
            %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/a/2087/381"} some theorem by Neeman
            to this effect.
          %p
            See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "affine+variety#Cohomology"} affine variety – Cohomology
          %h3#okagrauert_principle Oka-Grauert principle
          %p
            The
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oka-Grauert+principle"} Oka-Grauert principle
              states that for any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stein+manifold"} Stein manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              the holomorphic and the topological classification of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+bundles"} complex vector bundles
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              coincide. The original reference is (
              %a{:href => "#Grauert"}> Grauert 58
              ).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stein+site"} Stein site
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/EFC-algebra"} EFC-algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+analytic+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} complex analytic ∞-groupoid
          %h2#references References
          %p The original article:
          %ul
            %li#Stein51
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Karl+Stein"}> Karl Stein
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Analytische Funktionen mehrerer komplexer Veränderlichen zu vorgegebenen Periodizitätsmoduln und das zweite Cousinsche Problem
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02054949"} doi:10.1007/BF02054949
          %p Further development:
          %ul
            %li#Grauert58
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans+Grauert"}> Hans Grauert
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Analytische Faserungen über holomorph-vollständigen Räumen
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 135
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/BF01351803"} doi:10.1007/BF01351803
          %p
            Relation to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surfaces"} Riemann surfaces
          %ul
            %li#BehnkeStein47
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heinrich+Behnke"}> Heinrich Behnke
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Karl+Stein"} Karl Stein
              = succeed "," do
                %em Entwicklung analytischer Funktionen auf Riemannschen Flächen
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01447838"} doi:10.1007/BF01447838
          %p
            On how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+de+Rham+cohomology"} holomorphic de Rham cohomology
            on Stein manifolds coincides with ordinary
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Pierre+Serre"}> Jean-Pierre Serre
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quelques problemes globaux relatifs aux varietes de Stein
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-39816-2_23"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-39816-2_23
            %li#Serre54
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Pierre+Serre"}> Jean-Pierre Serre
                \: §2.1 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Cohomologie et fonctions de variables complexes
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/SB_1951-1954__2__213_0"} numdam:SB_1951-1954__2__213_0
          %p Texbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li#GrauertRemmert04
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans+Grauert"}> Hans Grauert
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reinhold+Remmert"} Reinhold Remmert
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Theory of Stein Spaces
            %li#Forstneric11
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Franc+Forstneri%C4%8D"}> Franc Forstnerič
                , Section 5.3 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Stein manifolds and holomorphic mappings – The homotopy principle in complex analysis
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-642-22250-4"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-22250-4
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (in relation to the
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oka+principle"} Oka principle
          %p Lecture notes:
          %ul
            %li#Wehler20
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joachim+Wehler"}> Joachim Wehler
              , Section 15 in:
              %em Riemann surfaces
              2020 (
              %a{:href => "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~wehler/20190530_RiemannSurfacesScript.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stein_manifold"} Stein manifold
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eom"}> eom
                ,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://www.encyclopediaofmath.org/index.php/Stein_space"} Stein space
            %li#Maddock09
              %p
                Zachary Maddock,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Dolbeault cohomology
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/MaddockDolbeault09.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p Discussion of the relation to affine varieties includes
          %ul
            %li#Zhang06
              Jing Zhang,
              %em Algebraic Stein Varieties
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0610886"}> arXiv:math/0610886
              )
          %p
            In relation to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy-theoretic
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oka+principle"} Oka principle
          %ul
            %li#Forstneric11
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Franc+Forstneri%C4%8D"}> Franc Forstnerič
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Stein manifolds and holomorphic mappings – The homotopy principle in complex analysis
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-642-22250-4"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-22250-4
          %p
            As a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+complex+analytic+geometry"} higher complex analytic geometry
            the category of Stein manifolds appears in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              , section 4.4. of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Structured+Spaces"} Structured Spaces
